 uring-an-active-war-lessons-from-the-ukraine-war-archive
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/building-evidentiary-infrastr
 ucture-during-an-active-war-lessons-from-the-ukraine-war-archive\n\nRussia
 's full-scale invasion of Ukraine has become the most extensively document
 ed war in history\, generating millions of photographs\, videos\, witness 
 testimonies and open-source records every day. Preserving this material is
  no longer simply an archival challenge: it is fundamental to future justi
 ce\, historical memory and understanding contemporary wars. In this sessio
 n\, Maksym Demydenko\, Co-Founder and Executive Director of the Ukraine Wa
 r Archive (UWA)\, will explore how UWA has built one of the world's larges
 t independent digital archives documenting the war in Ukraine. Drawing on 
 practical experience\, he will discuss the challenges of preserving digita
 l evidence during an ongoing conflict\, from provenance\, metadata and int
 eroperability to AI-assisted evidence management and the ethical responsib
 ilities of maintaining sensitive records at scale. The session will examin
 e how archives are evolving from passive repositories into active evidenti
 ary infrastructures that support investigators\, journalists and future ac
 countability processes. It will also consider how the Ukrainian experience
  can help archivists and records professionals prepare for future digital 
 conflicts.\n\nThis event is only open to Stanford students\, staff\, facul
 ty & affiliates.\n\nIn-person RSVP here.\nVirtual webinar registration her
 e. \n\nMaksym Demydenko is Co-Founder and Executive Director of the Ukrain
 e War Archive (UWA)\, an independent non-profit organisation dedicated to 
 preserving digital evidence of Russ
